      "result": {
        "body": "The USB debugging tools currently live in the giulia device repository. This brings their host-side terminal into the shared tree so compatible device ports can capture logs, issue bounded diagnostics, and use the framebuffer viewer from the same checkout.\n\n- Add the Python terminal, Windows CMD launcher, pinned pySerial dependency, GSC1 decoder, PNG capture, and localhost screen/input viewer.\n- Preserve protocol 2 diagnostics, explicit MMIO commands, PTY transitions, and acknowledged Fastboot reset handling. Require an explicit port or complete VID/PID/serial identity instead of hard-coded Ace 5 discovery.\n- Restrict periodic polling to read-only status queries, including a regression test for GPU HFI and input commands that must not be repeated automatically.\n- Add bilingual usage/compatibility documentation and a Python 3.10/3.14 test matrix on Windows, Linux, and macOS.\n\nDevice-side drivers, boot arguments, GPU bring-up changes, and shell installation remain in the device repository, following CONTRIBUTING.md. The initial backend retains the GIULIA protocol markers and GSC1 limits; selecting another USB identity does not make an unrelated kernel compatible. Source: tycykp/UniXNU-OnePlus-Ace5 at 694b39e plus the local terminal/screen work.\n\nValidation on Windows / Python 3.14.6:\n- `python -m unittest discover -s tests/terminal -v`: 45 tests, 44 passed; the optional device C-sender fixture test was skipped because its fixture was not provided.\n- `python scripts/debug_terminal.py --help` and `--list`: passed.\n- `git diff --cached --check`: passed before commit.\n- `npm ci --ignore-scripts` and `npm run build` in docs: passed (existing dependency audit and bundle-size warnings).\n- Workflow YAML parsed successfully; cross-platform execution is delegated to the added CI matrix.\n\nNo hardware commands were sent during this integration. PTY shell hardware validation remains outstanding; the documentation distinguishes host tests from device readiness.\r\n",
